lly  um  avoid and i leaned into those feelings  and i guess  took a more kind of  i i don't know um  immersive approach and those were days  where i was just kind of like headphone  headphones on like sulking in a corner  until they rolled  until the cameras started rolling um  but i think jonathan sometimes took this  scenes um at least my scenes  even harder than i did i really felt  kind of  the empathy of  tucker and his character on some of the  harder days  on set  yeah i think i mean you know you want to  throw yourself  into the character and the situation  um  but  when the content is so upsetting  um  it's it's  uh you know you're you're really  fighting between these two  forces  and  um i certainly wrestled with that fought  passionately  with those um different perspectives  and  you know to the credit of lillian and  jamie they were really helpful in just  allowing me to not  feel as  kind of scared and vulnerable  as i i was and had been i think the the  this  there's there's one or two scenes that i  found profoundly  challenging and jamie  was  concerned that i wasn't talking to her  because i was mad at her  i was just  a little too upset by the whole thing to  go back to the traditional convivial  conversations that we have been having  so i love learning about the behind the  scenes of the making of a movie and so  for soon to be fans of the film for each  of you what do you think they might be  surprised to learn about the actual  making of the film i'd say my my 2008  ford crew victoria p 71 police intercept  wasn't just  a stock standard transportation uh  mobile for a number of cast members and  crew but it was also a warming van it  was a place of solace for lily and i and  and it's featured prominently uh from  time to time in the film  yeah good behind the scenes like and  every time you see my crown vic you can  throw back a shot yes  we shot a real working motel um that uh  feels right out of uh you know the a  book like um  uh  nickel 